    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $n$ be a positive integer. Show that if $n$ is divisible by 3, then $n^2$ is divisible by three.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=3m$ gives $n^2=3(3m^2)$, so $n^2$ is divisible by 3.",
          "worked": "If $n$ is divisible by 3, write $n=3m$ for an integer $m$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=(3m)^2\\\\n^2&=9m^2\\\\n^2&=3(3m^2).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $3m^2$ is an integer, $n^2$ is divisible by 3.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $n$ is not divisible by 3, then $n^2$ is not divisible by 3. Hint: There are two cases to consider: $n=3k+1$ and $n=3k+2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $n=3k+1$ or $n=3k+2$, $n^2$ has remainder 1 when divided by 3. Hence it is not divisible by 3.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $n=3k+1$ for an integer $k$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=(3k+1)^2\\\\n^2&=9k^2+6k+1\\\\n^2&=3(3k^2+2k)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nCase 2: $n=3k+2$ for an integer $k$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=(3k+2)^2\\\\n^2&=9k^2+12k+4\\\\n^2&=3(3k^2+4k+1)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nIn each case, $n^2$ has remainder 1 when divided by 3, so it is not divisible by 3.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nA rational representation in lowest terms would force both numerator and denominator to be divisible by 3.",
          "worked": "Suppose $\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{a}{b}$ in lowest terms, where $a,b$ are positive integers.\n$$a^2=3b^2.$$\nThus $a^2$ is divisible by 3. By part (b), $a$ is divisible by 3, so write $a=3k$ for an integer $k$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(3k)^2&=3b^2\\\\9k^2&=3b^2\\\\b^2&=3k^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $b^2$ is divisible by 3, and part (b) gives that $b$ is divisible by 3. Both $a$ and $b$ therefore have a common factor of 3, contradicting the lowest-terms assumption. Thus $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
